{"title":"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 7","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Less_Convoluted_Solution_4)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Less Convoluted Solution 4)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(Infinite_Geometric_Sums)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (Infinite Geometric Sums)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(basically_steps_of_solution_4_but_different_math)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (basically steps of solution 4 but different math)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Note_on_evaluation_of_the_series\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Note on evaluation of the series</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_MadMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by MadMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A standard fair six-sided die is rolled repeatedly. Each time the die reads 1 or 2, Alice gets a coin; each time it reads 3 or 4, Bob gets a coin; and each time it reads 5 or 6, Carol gets a coin. The probability that Alice and Bob each receive at least two coins before Carol receives any coins can be written as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/c/e8cbebf38fa12c5a31ea7748b27b06ebd1c82da2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\tfrac mn,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"23\" height=\"20\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> are relatively prime positive integers. Find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/0/08026d6dd55c82a98b78283329b9e572ae24d906.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100m+n.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"83\" height=\"15\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Denote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> for Alice, Bob, and Carol respectively. Consider the situation after three rolls.\n</p><p><b>Case 1.</b> One of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> received two coins and the other received one, which occurs with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/7/1b72412a5837fac77bb0abdc0fd19757deeb70c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> since there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> ways to permute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/5/d/75d56bbc7436d914fa54dbe0300e5829c0091304.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AAB$\" width=\"41\" height=\"13\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/2/902599f84f3793aa342eb283546401c500b6b812.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BBA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/5/1051e7d41ec0b8bd688a872be17538413f4ee97b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> ways to choose three letters in total. Then there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> that the player who received one will receive another before <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> does while ignoring the third player.\n</p><p><b>Case 2.</b> One of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> received three coins and the other received none, which occurs with probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cd697bc48dddbba36512f43d5a0cfe10e07278.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> due to there being only two cases, namely <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/6/fb679a47215af5398fac903b3c7a30f5f6f935a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AAA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/1/021dba1da04c3f1738fe7f57075582fd77466441.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BBB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. Then, there is a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/7/3b7621b32176df652881029b9aa4ddf61f975434.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"41\" height=\"38\" /> chance of the second player receiving both coins before <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> does while ignoring the third player.\n</p><p>The total probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/c/4bcc62bb2f24d62e0cc5edf1bd05d8abaeac0bed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{6}{27}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{2}{27}\\cdot\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{1}{9}+\\frac{1}{54}=\\frac{7}{54}\\implies\\boxed{754}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"337\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Eevee9406\">eevee9406</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/6/2d681b85deed0dc65b5d2134f50636c3d11e9825.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(a,b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"52\" height=\"18\" /> be the probability that Alice and Bob recieve two coins before Carol receives any, given that Alice and Bob respectively start with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> coins. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> be a placeholder for all integers greater than two. Also, by symmetry, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/0/fa06e3ba715f278233c3277140e7db6eb1feb0ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(a,b)=P(b,a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"134\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>We find that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/e/b9ed5f486a39fef6620b6b411a82d6ae25c8f4e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(2,2)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"90\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/7/4d7ebfe7bb3980d0410a9cdc48fca40ae0d38397.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(2,1) = \\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"38\" /> by symmetry. Then \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/8/3d890a94fe816150889f260007429750ff21509c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(2,0) = \\frac{1}{3}P(2,0) + \\frac{1}{3}P(2,1),\\]\" width=\"240\" height=\"37\" />\nand we find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/8/1985acf141569d31bc968b83810a74004a73fc87.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(2,0)=\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"38\" />. Next, we have \n\\begin{align*}\nP(1,1) &amp;= \\frac{2}{3}P(2,1) = \\frac{1}{3},\\\\\nP(1,0) &amp;= \\frac{1}{3}P(2,0)+\\frac{1}{3}P(1,1) = \\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{1}{9} = \\frac{7}{36}.\n\\end{align*}\nFinally <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/6/7e6c43edda362300c74144772debe1331cc567a6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(0,0) = \\frac{2}{3}P(1,0)=\\frac{7}{54}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"195\" height=\"38\" /> and the requested sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/44877965a8472a732513e66144ec34e93fd527d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{754}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"40\" height=\"25\" />. ~TThB0501\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Less_Convoluted_Solution_4.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Less_Convoluted_Solution_4)\">Solution 3 (Less Convoluted Solution 4)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Less Convoluted Solution 4)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Consider the ordering of the first four draws. We must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/d/5bd3679bf995bed65535870ee3b94b8e498b653f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AABB$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" />, or some permutation of such, giving us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/c/33c8b1f2a1f82250b4bbf36b162c9f772257bf1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4!}{2!2!} = \\binom{4}{2} = 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"129\" height=\"55\" /> total configurations. The string has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/c/6ec2ade6c7b9bccb6725a78b0d22541bb9fa91b4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1/3)^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"51\" height=\"21\" /> probability of occurring, multiplying by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> total strings gives us the index probability <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/b/d0bf7e55ed399ebb01f33e15180a607cca7a830b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6/81$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/1/4c16ae4b6ffaf141620c0fca027a3cde8a4e5655.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2/27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Now, from this index, we have four states. Given that Carlos rolls next, for more than five strings (four from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/d/5bd3679bf995bed65535870ee3b94b8e498b653f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AABB$\" width=\"56\" height=\"13\" /> rolls and one from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> roll),\n</p><p>- Either we draw <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> (or vice versa)\n</p><p>- We draw <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>- We draw <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>From this, we can easily continue with series. To solve the first point, notice that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/c/08ce4ccc8f0624befaa9ce7855fc1afc69b8e5ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2!$\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> orderings to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" />. Multiplying by the probability of this <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> string, which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/3/0d3dba22edcee4ff7213635505e90128beebb42e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />, to the index and every other plausible index with the inclusion of the roll Carlos pertains results in:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/3/0b33de07b2a31c174d98538b1a314bca2230e706.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2! \\cdot \\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\left(\\frac{2}{27}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{9}\\right)^k\\]\" width=\"195\" height=\"50\" />\nThe sum is an infinite geometric series with first term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/d/00d308942b9316e22299e280711e5044fb4d692d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 2/81$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"72\" height=\"20\" /> and common ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/3/0d3dba22edcee4ff7213635505e90128beebb42e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" />, through the geometric series formula is a sum:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/2/5526b4319021746d1348af5cff1dba4f9d3cf62e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2/81}{1 - 1/9} = \\frac{2/81}{8/9} = \\frac{2}{81} \\cdot \\frac{9}{8} = \\frac{2}{9} \\cdot \\frac{1}{8} = \\frac{2}{72} = \\frac{1}{36}\\]\" width=\"352\" height=\"43\" />\nMultiplication by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/9/c09fdb545c8e1cacab5909d59e3fd851bc343160.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{36} \\cdot 2 = \\frac{1}{18}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"93\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Then, for the second and third points, notice the symmetry between the two. Thus, we solve for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />, and multiply by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to account for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. The chance of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> happening is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/7/217aedbdc339bacc8ba075a2ec16902b098194e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" />, and we apply this to the index, along with Carlos' probability, obtaining:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/4/e048f7eb5fdd9ec13228d93a25772e4c1e2331dc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\left(\\frac{2}{27}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)^k\\]\" width=\"168\" height=\"50\" />\nThis is an infinite geometric series with first term <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/d/68d6e42f8641445e219fc8e8d3e78d25d04c3b92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2/81$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" />, and common ratio <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/7/217aedbdc339bacc8ba075a2ec16902b098194e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" />, and solving via the formula obtains:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/4/b74ba454fba00d2c934df21afc2fe32eb2b55ac6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{2/81}{1 - 1/3} = \\frac{2/81}{2/3} = \\frac{2}{81} \\cdot \\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{3}{81} = \\frac{1}{27}\\]\" width=\"289\" height=\"43\" />\nMultiplication by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> gives us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/1/4c16ae4b6ffaf141620c0fca027a3cde8a4e5655.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2/27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"38\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>The answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/4/9d4cfea8a41c8321428d8fbcdbf775384920afaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{18} + \\frac{2}{27}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"68\" height=\"38\" />, which we can solve through LCD, obtaining a probability of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/7/96782fce5eee3cdc8b00ff0dc7c48f547220dcdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{54} + \\frac{4}{54} = \\frac{7}{54}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"115\" height=\"38\" />, which is in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/c/83ce0c4966858ed8ef848e596e2bd7e8f694dc81.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m/n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"35\" height=\"18\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/5/78535d0b3120e26aa7ebfc02aeae28e5f0809201.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m, n) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"18\" />. Thus the answer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/3/e63ccaf9dcb29b8ccce3180b0c8f0b9ece7fd9f3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100m + n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"78\" height=\"15\" /> is nothing but <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/5/4f5484ebe513fcc0fa7c0068df4d0794f254275d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$700 + 54 = \\boxed{754}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"132\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28Infinite_Geometric_Sums.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(Infinite_Geometric_Sums)\">Solution 4 (Infinite Geometric Sums)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (Infinite Geometric Sums)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The problem is asking for the probability that both Alice and Bob have 2 coins before Carol gets her first coin. Thus, we only care about what happens before and up to the point where both Alice and Bob have 2 coins.\n</p><p>So, once we see that both Alice and Bob have two coins, we stop checking.\n</p><p>We can check all series of coin flip outcomes from length 4 to infinity.\nLet's first assume Alice gets to 2 coins first, then Bob.\nLength 4: Must be some form of ABAB -&gt; last B is fixed, there are 3 ways to arrange the ABA, this has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/1/d/c1d44578698cf00916e48861d55d19b40ea9ef73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}^4=\\frac{1}{81}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"69\" height=\"42\" /> chance of happening -&gt; chance that it takes 4 tries for both Alice and Bob to have at least 2 coins is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/7/6677a82459adaf5f8047dfbd50961e7e9f6c33f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{81}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"29\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Length 5: Must be some form of ABAAB -&gt; Last B is fixed, there are 4 ways to arrange the ABAA, this has a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/f/5bfb1e66dc77e4050a3fd5af974b15f5fee08235.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{3}^5 = \\frac{1}{243}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"78\" height=\"42\" /> chance of happening -&gt; chance is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/6/fe6cb79d61d4cff77e29102af24e7f8123f9fb4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{4}{243}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"38\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>In general, for a sequence of length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/5/4/c54e7152acd659788861f0453ac195c635e0a005.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"18\" height=\"13\" /> the probability is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/7/cd799f9a4d897174c135609bb5d21565d5b8e25b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n-1}{3^n},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"53\" height=\"38\" /> so our final probability sum will look something like this:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/a/70aa9ef53e16860e6fa6fbed7a3a0cbe0cb815df.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P = \\frac{3}{81}+\\frac{4}{243}+\\frac{5}{729}+\\frac{6}{2187}+...\\]\" width=\"265\" height=\"38\" />\nWe can break the individual components of P after the first term down into 3 and several \"1 bits\":\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/3/6b3ae96018467fafba22551eb79ce9830a7c083f.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{4}{243} = \\frac{3}{243}+\\frac{1}{243}\\]\" width=\"136\" height=\"37\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/5/a95c279e42f283563e55ffe3153906f808ee1153.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{5}{729} = \\frac{3}{729}+\\frac{1}{729}+\\frac{1}{729}\\]\" width=\"190\" height=\"38\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa25a0a998da46bb93b7e11b07ee724d4186896a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{6}{2187} = \\frac{3}{2187} + \\frac{1}{2187} + \\frac{1}{2187} + \\frac{1}{2187}\\]\" width=\"289\" height=\"37\" />\nSo, from this, we can take first the sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/1/c91981be404b90aa52c825595674af9cc8d456be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S_1 = \\frac{3}{81}+\\frac{3}{243}+\\frac{3}{729}+\\frac{3}{2187}+... = \\frac{3}{81}\\cdot\\frac{3}{2} = \\frac{1}{18}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"398\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Then, we can do<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/7/7/277da911af1b30cf1c51714e76de2d911210691d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S_2 =(\\frac{1}{243}+\\frac{1}{729}+\\frac{1}{2187} + \\frac{1}{6561}+...) + (\\frac{1}{729}+\\frac{1}{2187}+\\frac{1}{6561}+...) + (\\frac{1}{2187} + \\frac{1}{6561}+...)+...\\]\" width=\"649\" height=\"37\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/c/ffc2f9f602e9bf83a5df73cb443ea90c04be238c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S_2 = \\frac{1}{243}\\cdot\\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{1}{729}\\cdot\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{1}{2187}\\cdot\\frac{3}{2}+...\\]\" width=\"304\" height=\"37\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/1/5017e4c6a1fdb0b8cec967343b9d43a6350c36a8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S_2 = \\frac{1}{162} + \\frac{1}{486} + \\frac{1}{1458} + ...\\]\" width=\"225\" height=\"37\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/9/f4910514cd9a9c091d41023add472b27540d8dd3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S_2 = \\frac{1}{162}\\cdot\\frac{3}{2}\\]\" width=\"98\" height=\"37\" /><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/1/53165af7251b263cb11964cfc719f8f63a1c1417.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[S_2 = \\frac{1}{108}.\\]\" width=\"77\" height=\"37\" />\nThus, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/2/6f200d7b0707289fa3dea886b8aef090ec6c4395.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P = S_1+S_2 = \\frac{1}{18} + \\frac{1}{108} = \\frac{7}{108}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"260\" height=\"38\" />\n</p><p>Note that we just talked about the case where Bob gets the last coin. But note that the case where Alice gets the last coin is symmetrical to this, and thus our answer is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/6/746dab7fa8a99035652e1585a875b4f7198fcb13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2P = \\frac{7}{54},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"77\" height=\"38\" /> and thus our requested answer would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/2/c92bb255010600e5b9a3a57e2d019672dcfe06f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{754}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"45\" height=\"25\" />\n-DuoDuoling0\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28basically_steps_of_solution_4_but_different_math.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(basically_steps_of_solution_4_but_different_math)\">Solution 5 (basically steps of solution 4 but different math)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (basically steps of solution 4 but different math)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Like solution 4 we will stop the sequence after getting two of each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are symmetric in terms of probability so lets first tackle the case where it ends in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<ul><li><b>Case 1:</b> String of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/4/494d1ffa421df4227f586a02939ee88e603eff3b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B \\dots A B \\dots A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"113\" height=\"14\" /> where the strings of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />'s are of length 1 or more.</li></ul>\n<dl><dd>The chance of this is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/8/9386eedb612267daf2c690246a1e01483bfc296a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(P(B \\dots)) \\cdot (P(A)) \\cdot (P(B \\dots)) \\cdot (P(A))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"322\" height=\"20\" />.</dd>\n<dd>The chance of a string of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />'s can be evaluated using a geometric series:</dd>\n<dd><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/8/f487644dc26eaaa839c8a3703f0ec76f6faebbf7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(B) = 1/3, \\quad P(BB) = 1/9, \\quad P(BBB) = 1/27 \\dots$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"415\" height=\"20\" /></dd>\n<dd>Add all of these up and you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/3/1f3cc0ad966b3e637cd199607cbf0d1befc0e491.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"29\" height=\"20\" />.</dd>\n<dd>The total probability for this case is:</dd>\n<dd><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/4/f64ee5f7ae4c25a895e16ccb1b60cae5616f3e85.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1/2) \\cdot (2/6) \\cdot (1/2) \\cdot (2/6) = 1/36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"267\" height=\"20\" /></dd></dl>\n<ul><li><b>Case 2 &amp; 3:</b> There are 2 more cases, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/1/c/61c03f61d6e16149de814ccc46f60518778296e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABB \\dots A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76ba84704c1978b38de929daeaf0db8ad0f30181.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BB \\dots AA$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" /> where the strings of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />'s is of length 2 or more.</li></ul>\n<dl><dd><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/a/59a79e6e758a74d288dd720526d0a532e4144173.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(BB) = 1/9, \\quad P(BBB) = 1/27 \\dots \\implies P(BB \\dots) = 1/6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"479\" height=\"20\" /></dd>\n<dd>So the total probability is:</dd>\n<dd><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/6/6f6da72904c314a4abc545dde5ec9b45b5f8938d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2/6)(1/6)(2/6) + (1/6)(2/6)(2/6) = 1/54 + 1/54 = 1/27$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"452\" height=\"20\" /></dd></dl>\n<p><b>Final Calculation:</b>\n</p>\n<ul><li>Chance of string ending in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> is equal to:</li></ul>\n<dl><dd><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/1/701b82f32b0a7d5c18e71db5a9901c6d7d968e98.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1/27 + 1/36 = 7/108$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"166\" height=\"20\" /></dd></dl>\n<ul><li>Chance of ending in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> is:</li></ul>\n<dl><dd><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/7/107f89c0ea4e2e10bc0710bce99e25c8217d092b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$7/108 \\cdot 2 = 7/54$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"130\" height=\"20\" /></dd></dl>\n<p>The requested answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/d/aed5e7881267601c619fb5dbd563674051dd5c67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100 \\cdot 7 + 54 = \\mathbf{754}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"147\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n-Olecram(hopefully someone can make it into latex and format it)\n</p><p>-LI,CHENXI (no problem bro )\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Note_on_evaluation_of_the_series\">Note on evaluation of the series</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Note on evaluation of the series\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Sometimes, one may feel like one does not want to perform such careful arithmetico-geometrico manipulation. Note that the answer is essentially\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/c/edceb3909ba722f8aaee893efdcf9c6b9ed827c7.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2 \\sum_{k=2}^\\infty (k+1) \\left( \\frac{1}{3} \\right)^{k+2} = \\frac{2}{9} \\left( \\sum_{k=2}^\\infty k x^k + \\sum_{k=2}^\\infty x^k \\right)\\]\" width=\"351\" height=\"53\" />\n</p><p>and the first sum can be evaluated using differentiation of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/7/b1704324e626cf3ef7f044c7d5cc466194578084.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{1-x} = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty x^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"120\" height=\"53\" />, with the second being exactly this sum. Thus, we can simply plug and chug to get the final answer with this (instead of being really careful with arithmetico geometrico)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_MadMath\">Video Solution by MadMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by MadMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/Mb6fq2DiUrU\">https://youtu.be/Mb6fq2DiUrU</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_7&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II\" title=\"2026 AIME II\">2026 AIME II</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Answer_Key\" title=\"2026 AIME II Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2026\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 6\">Problem 6</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 8\">Problem 8</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_AIME_II_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2026 AIME II Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflin